      "writtenOn": "2022-08-09T04:32:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is already in, but IIUC, this means you can invoke with something like\n\n    ./gen_jarjar.py --jars a.jar b.jar c.jar --prefix xyz\n\nIf that\u0027s true, it\u0027s very unconventional and conflicts with normal usage ; in normal argument usage this is supposed to be the same as\n\n    ./gen_jarjar.py b.jar c.jar --jars a.jar --prefix xyz\n\nEven the very rare programs where order of with-dash arguments matter with respect to without-dash arguments (e.g. ImageMagick\u0027s `convert`) will understand `--option word1 word2` as word2 being a no-dash argument and absolutely not a second argument to `--option`.\n\nI also don\u0027t understand how this works with `\u003d` (presumably ArgumentParser knows that `--prefix\u003dxyz` is equivalent to `--prefix xyz` and supports this).\n\nAs really the jars are what this script operates on, I think it would make most sense to simply remove \"--jars\" and have the no-dash args be the jars. Other --options are normally simply repeated (e.g. `--apistubs a.txt --apistubs b.txt`)\n\nAnother solution would be to have a separator like `,` or `;` and you\u0027d write `--jars\u003da.jar,b.jar,c.jar`. Drawback is, the separator is forbidden in file names, but maybe that doesn\u0027t matter a lot. We could also have the jars the no-dash arguments and use a separator for other args if you don\u0027t like repeating the --option.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 32,
        "startChar": 38,
        "endLine": 32,
        "endChar": 88
      },
      "revId": "cc9c6b7a0fea248228b9c408137990ad1b82caf4",
